and that's going to take up the remainder of the podcast.

66
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:16,240
So let's get started, shall we?

67
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:35,680
So here's an aha moment.

68
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:38,400
It's an aha moment for one of our scouts

69
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:40,400
and for one of our scouters.

70
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:43,760
These are great moments and I know you've had a few of them

71
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:46,720
and I hope you have many, many more.

72
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:50,160
Here's a friend of the blog and the podcast, Mike Marty

73
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:54,960
Hey Clark. this is Mike Marty, with Troop 4318 in Adamtown, Pennsylvania,

74
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,200
and I would like to share a scouting story with you.

75
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:02,400
This week, my SPL came to me asking me how he could better connect

76
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:04,080
with one of the younger scouts in our troop.

77
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:07,200
At first I thought the SPL was simply complaining,

78
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,760
because all of the things he was talking about were negatives

79
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,640
On how the boy can't sit still to save his life,

80
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:14,640
on how he rarely focuses

81
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:18,320
and on how he often interrupts other scouts

82
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:19,840
when they're trying to complete tasks.

83
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:22,800
The longer I listened to my SPL, though,

84
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:25,920
the more I realized that he was truly being sincere.

85
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:28,640
He was trying to figure out a way to connect with a scout.

86
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:33,760
When I mentioned to the SPL that this scout is 11 years old

87
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:34,640
and he's autistic,

88
00:05:35,440 --> 00:05:37,760
the SPL immediately fired back at me.

89
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:40,320
but we have other autistic boys in the troop.

90
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:41,520
my boys are autistic.

91
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:44,400
But then I asked my SPL to consider:

92
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:47,760
every one of our autistic boys is unique.

93
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:49,760
He comes to us with his own gifts,

94
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:52,000
his own opportunities and his own challenges.

95
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:55,200
Give me three things that you like about this scout.

96
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:58,320
Immediately, my SPL fired back again.

97
00:05:59,039 --> 00:06:00,000
He likes to camp-

98
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,599
He's always the first one there-

99
00:06:01,599 --> 00:06:03,520
and asked to go camping more often.

100
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:06,000
He loves to hike and he loves to play games.

101
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:08,480
So then I asked the SPL:

102
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:11,120
where in there do you find commonality?

103
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,200
And then the SPL said,

104
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:14,880
hey, I like to play games too.

105
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:18,240
And then all of a sudden, a light clicked off in his head

106
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:20,080
And I said: yeah,

107
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:22,080
I said that's the place that you want to meet him.

108
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:26,880
It's truly inspiring to see these young men grow up.

109
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,240
My SPL has been in the troop for the last six years

110
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:33,680
and he has truly overcome some of his own challenges

111
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:35,760
to turn into a fine young scout.

112
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:38,320
But the thing that admires me the most about him

113
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,880
is his sincerity when given the opportunity

114
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:44,159
to really try to fix a problem on his own.

115
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:46,880
Thank you for what you do, Clark,

116
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:48,560
and for helping me become a better leader.

117
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:02,320
You can send me your story too.

118
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:03,680
It's not hard to do.

119
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:05,360
There's a link in the podcast notes

120
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:06,960
that'll help you make that happen.

121
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:19,200
Email, that is folks.

122
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:22,960
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

123
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:33,680
Andy Davis is the scout master of Troop 43,

124
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:35,680
and he wrote to ask me this question.

125
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:40,720
Our troop is preparing to grow from 18 to 28 scouts

126
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:41,600
in the next couple of weeks.

127
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:43,600
Well, that's a pretty good problem to have.

128
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:45,840
The dilemma is we're not too sure

129
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:48,560
how we should handle the 10 new scouts joining,

130
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:51,760
Should we split them into separate new scout patrols

131
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:53,200
or keep them as one unit

132
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:55,520
until they've been in the troop for about four to six months?

133
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:58,080
If their participation level is low,

134
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:00,080
it would seem one patrol would make more sense.

135
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:02,240
However, if participation is high,

136
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:04,080
having two patrols might be a better option.

137
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:05,760
Of course, with two patrols

138
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:09,360
we'd also need an additional new scout patrol, assistant

139
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:12,160
scout master and troop guide.

140
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:15,040
What wisdom or best practice can you provide

141
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:16,880
that may help us weigh our decision?

142
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:20,240
Again, much appreciation for all the fantastic advice

143
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:22,160
and commentary you bring to the scouting community.

144
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:25,200
Well, thank you, Andy, for your kind words.

145
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:30,160
This is a pretty common question I get in one form or another,

146
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:33,840
And my general advice when it comes to thinking about patrols

147
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:35,600
has two basic points.

148
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:38,080
Number one: what you think and what I think

149
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:39,360
and what most scouts think

150
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:41,360
is important about patrols isn't.

151
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:42,480
So to begin with,

152
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:45,120
try forgetting everything you know about patrols.

153
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:48,480
No troop guides, no patrol assistant scout masters-

154
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:50,080
These things are pretty meaningless

155
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:52,320
to the scouts who are going to be in the patrol.

156
00:08:52,320 --> 00:08:53,840
The second bit of advice is

157
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:55,840
get your patrol leaders counsel together

158
00:08:55,840 --> 00:08:57,760
and ask what they'd like to see happen

159
00:08:57,760 --> 00:08:59,120
and how they'd like to arrange things

160
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:00,560
with these 10 new scouts?

161
00:09:00,560 --> 00:09:03,520
No, preconceived. this is the way we've always done it.

162
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:04,960
Ask them some questions.

163
00:09:04,960 --> 00:09:06,560
They get them thinking about this

164
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:09,200
and have them come up with a strategy.

165
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:13,440
Ask what a patrol is, why we have patrols

166
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:15,280
and what they see as being the best way

167
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:16,720
to handle the current situation.

168
00:09:17,440 --> 00:09:19,440
What are they going to ask the new scouts?

169
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:21,760
that will help them decide what to do.

170
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:25,120
What's interesting here is a very basic principle

171
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:28,400
that I think applies to just about everything we do in scouting,

172
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:31,199
And that's the difference between leveraging

173
00:09:31,199 --> 00:09:33,680
those kind of natural things

174
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:35,520
that scouting is based on

175
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:37,840
or setting them up artificially.

176
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:39,520
One of the more interesting experiences

177
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:41,199
that really taught me a lot about that

178
00:09:41,199 --> 00:09:43,280
was attending a national jamboree,

179
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:44,880
And this was ages ago.

180
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:46,800
People really love those big events.

181
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:49,280
It's not really something that I'm all that into,

182
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:50,800
But it taught me a couple of things

183
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:52,720
that I think were important,

184
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:54,800
Because when you talk about putting together

185
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:55,840
a jamboree troop,

186
00:09:55,840 --> 00:09:58,319
it's a totally artificial construct.

187
00:09:58,320 --> 00:10:00,640
The adult volunteers are selected

188
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:03,760
according to whatever criteria they're selected,

189
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:07,680
The scouts are apportioned off into jamboree troops

190
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:09,360
in whatever way that that happens,

191
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:11,440
And then it's left up to the adults

192
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:15,040
to try and establish those scouts into patrols,

193
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:19,040
And my experience with that was really problematic.

194
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:20,480
We did our best to try.

195
00:10:20,480 --> 00:10:23,040
We went on a couple of shakedown campouts

196
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:26,640
and we got to kind of look at what these guys were doing

197
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:29,120
And I remember lots and lots of papers.

198
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:32,240
I remember forms filled out by the scouters

199
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:34,800
who were in the scout's home unit.

200
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:37,120
We were supposed to use that information

201
00:10:37,120 --> 00:10:40,800
in our observations during the couple of shakedown campouts

202
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:43,600
to set up patrols and to appoint patrol leaders.

203
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:47,199
We had four patrols in our jamboree troop

204
00:10:47,199 --> 00:10:52,800
And I think probably one of them kind of functioned.

205
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:55,600
The other three were always a bit of a mess-

206
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:58,240
And I'm all about being a bit of a mess and things like that-

207
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:00,720
but they were a bit of a mess for the wrong reasons.

208
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:03,360
They were a bit of a mess because you had eight scouts

209
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:06,560
in those patrols who were assigned to those patrols

210
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:08,560
in a very artificial way. right,

211
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:10,480
It was probably the best way that it could have happened

212
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:12,160
for given the circumstances,

213
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:13,440
But it was still very artificial.

214
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:14,320
They never really clicked

215
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:15,920
and they never really got along very well

216
00:11:15,920 --> 00:11:20,240
for the first two thirds of the entire jamboree experience.

217
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:23,920
And that was because we had this kind of artificial setup

218
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:25,520
where the adults knew better

219
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:27,520
And we were going to make all those decisions,

220
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,600
And we were going to make all the decisions about this

221
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:31,439
and set everything up that way.

222
00:11:31,439 --> 00:11:33,760
It just proved not to be a very good idea,

223
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:36,880
And that'll prove out in your own unit too.

224
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:39,439
You don't want to look at the paperwork

225
00:11:39,439 --> 00:11:41,760
and do an artificial construct on things,

226
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:43,199
Because the real secret is

227
00:11:43,199 --> 00:11:46,079
is that you don't really have to make patrols at all.

228
00:11:46,079 --> 00:11:48,560
The scouts are making patrols for themselves.

229
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:50,800
You don't have to game their lineup.

230
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:52,560
They have their friendships,

231
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:55,040
And if you look at this from the perspective

232
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:56,240
of your new scout-

233
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:58,480
the new scouts that we're talking about here-

234
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:00,000
they have one thing on their minds

235
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
and that's the only thing that's actually important to them:

236
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:03,839
They want to be in a patrol with their friends,

237
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:07,120
Because scouting is all about getting to go camping

238
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:08,640
and do cool stuff with your friends.

239
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:10,079
That's what the whole thing is

240
00:12:10,079 --> 00:12:12,240
when you're 10 and a half or 11 years old.

241
00:12:12,240 --> 00:12:14,880
Now, if you decide that you're smarter than them

242
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:17,120
and you're going to make up their patrols,

243
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:19,199
you will learn.

244
00:12:19,199 --> 00:12:23,040
you'll learn that scouts generally ignore your decisions

245
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:25,920
and gravitate towards spending time with their friends anyway,

246
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:29,120
And what's kind of frustrating about that is

247
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:31,439
that newer scouts might sit down and figure

248
00:12:31,439 --> 00:12:35,040
where we'll make up our patrols and we'll figure all this out.

249
00:12:35,040 --> 00:12:37,040
And then they see that they've figured all this out

250
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:38,640
and the scouts kind of ignore the fact

251
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:39,839
that they're in a given patrol

252
00:12:39,839 --> 00:12:41,520
and they just hang out with their friends anyway

253
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:44,079
every chance that they get during a meeting

254
00:12:44,079 --> 00:12:45,520
or during a camp out.

255
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:47,120
And so we figure,

256
